                ],
                "senses": [
                    {
                        "sense_id": "5155eae9-ee48-3cef-8e49-7f466469c4cd",
                        "entry_id": "55bb4fd4-4430-3bc2-b8d5-5fd0707feb84",
                        "glosses": [
                            "Not self-conscious; natural and genuine."
                        ],
                        "tags": [],
                        "examples": [
                            {
                                "example_id": "9a7357da-22c9-41ec-a46a-641f43a31522",
                                "sense_id": "5155eae9-ee48-3cef-8e49-7f466469c4cd",
                                "text": "After a minute's silence he spoke again in Tibetan, in a voice creaky with age but curiously vibrant with the unselfconscious habit of authority."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "e4e66f93-63a0-404b-a858-c9fc36f382d5",
                                "sense_id": "5155eae9-ee48-3cef-8e49-7f466469c4cd",
                                "text": "The landscapes of group identity — the ethnoscapes — around the world are no longer familiar anthropological objects, insofar as groups are no longer tightly territorialized, spatially bounded, historically unselfconscious, or culturally homogeneous."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "fdbce6d3-d3e9-4f96-a9dc-bbef926d6771",
                                "sense_id": "5155eae9-ee48-3cef-8e49-7f466469c4cd",
                                "text": "In the lighted houses of the Meisners, of the Schumperts and the Persons and the Roots, people were clearly at home—whole families grouped around tables, young heads bent over homework, dens aflicker with TV, toddlers careening, a grandparent testing a tea bag’s virtue with a third soaking. These were spirited, unselfconscious houses."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "d592b3ef-b23d-457d-95a3-4e5d5d907a9f",
                                "sense_id": "5155eae9-ee48-3cef-8e49-7f466469c4cd",
                                "text": "Even the blithely unselfconscious Homer is more than a little freaked out by West’s private reverie, and encourages his spawn to move slowly away without making eye contact with the crazy man."
                            }
                        ]
                    },
                    {
                        "sense_id": "16f0bc11-1370-37ae-9615-876a892c2b8e",
                        "entry_id": "55bb4fd4-4430-3bc2-b8d5-5fd0707feb84",
                        "glosses": [
                            "Lacking a sense of self."
                        ],
                        "tags": [],
                        "examples": [
                            {
                                "example_id": "3956b5b6-7112-4746-a0e6-73be5de2920f",
                                "sense_id": "16f0bc11-1370-37ae-9615-876a892c2b8e",
                                "text": "In fact, if the quotation be read over again with this interpretation (which I do not say Wordsworth intended) that the 'birth' spoken of is the birth or evolution of the distinctively self-conscious Man from the Animals and the animal-natured, unself-conscious human beings of a preceding age, then the parable unfolds itself perfectly naturally and convincingly."
                            }
                        ]
                    }
                ],
                "translations": [
                    {
                        "translation_id": "ef5041a1-3c2b-4c28-9d8e-0ace54d97f35",
                        "source_entry_id": "55bb4fd4-4430-3bc2-b8d5-5fd0707feb84",
